export default {"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first day, for real","md":"This is the boss challenge for Base Camp. There is no new theory here: the challenge pulls together everything from this realm into one realistic scenario.\n\n**The scenario.** You start as a junior developer at *Lumen Studio* on Monday. IT handed you a fresh laptop. Before your 10 am stand-up you need to:\n\n1. Set up a tidy workspace folder.\n2. Configure git with your identity and the team's defaults.\n3. Take a look at the team's starter repository on GitHub and copy it to your machine.\n4. Check its recent history so you can say something sensible about the project at stand-up.\n\nTake a moment to recall the tools before you start:\n\n| Need | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Where am I / what is here | `pwd`, `ls -a` |\n| Make and enter folders | `mkdir -p`, `cd` |\n| Configure git | `git config --global key \"value\"` |\n| Read a GitHub repo | `gh repo view owner/name` |\n| Copy a repo | `git clone <url>` |\n| Read history | `git log --oneline` |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Boss labs give fewer hints than normal labs. Try each task on your own before you open a hint: retrieving an answer from memory is exactly what makes it stick."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Base Camp gave you","md":"- **Version control** keeps a complete, trustworthy history: what changed, who, when and why. Teams depend on it to work in parallel, review changes, deploy and undo.\n- **The terminal** is where git lives. Ten commands (`pwd`, `ls`, `cd`, `mkdir`, `touch`, `echo >`/`>>`, `cat`, `mv`, `cp`, `rm`) carry you a long way.\n- **Git** is the program on your machine. **GitHub** is a service that hosts git repositories and adds collaboration.\n- **Configure git** once per machine: `user.name`, `user.email`, `init.defaultBranch`, `core.editor`. Global settings live in `~/.gitconfig`; local ones in `.git/config` override them.\n\nNext realm: you create your own repository and make your first commits."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Monday, 9:15 am","md":"Complete the whole setup. The team's repository is `lumen-studio/starter-kit` on GitHub; you are a member of the organisation, so you can read it. Your workspace should be `~/code`."}},"basecamp/configure-git":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The commits signed by \"root\"","md":"Kofi's team noticed something odd in their history: dozens of commits by an author called `root <root@ubuntu-s-1vcpu>`. Nobody knew who that was. It turned out to be Kofi himself: he had been committing from a server where git had never been told his name, so it guessed from the machine.\n\nOn GitHub those commits were not linked to his profile, did not count towards his contribution graph, and when a bug turned up the team had to ask around to find out who wrote the code.\n\nThe fix took fifteen seconds:\n\n```term\n$ git config --global user.name \"Kofi Mensah\"\n$ git config --global user.email \"kofi@example.com\"\n```\n\nEvery commit you make carries a name and an email. Setting them is the very first thing to do on any machine where you use git."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Install, then configure","md":"**Install git**\n\n- **Windows**: download *Git for Windows* from git-scm.com. Accept the defaults, and use *Git Bash* as your terminal.\n- **macOS**: run `git --version` in Terminal; macOS offers to install the developer tools. Or `brew install git`.\n- **Linux**: `sudo apt install git` (Debian/Ubuntu) or `sudo dnf install git` (Fedora).\n\nCheck it worked:\n\n```term\n$ git --version\ngit version 2.47.0\n```\n\n**Configure it once per machine**\n\n```term\n$ git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\n$ git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\"\n$ git config --global init.defaultBranch main\n$ git config --global core.editor \"code --wait\"\n```\n\n| Setting | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `user.name` | Shown as the author of every commit |\n| `user.email` | Links commits to your GitHub account. Use an email that is on your GitHub profile (or GitHub's private `…@users.noreply.github.com` address). |\n| `init.defaultBranch` | New repositories start on `main` instead of git's old default `master` |\n| `core.editor` | The editor git opens when it needs you to write text, such as a commit message |\n\n**Read your configuration**\n\n```term\n$ git config user.name          # one value\n$ git config --list             # everything\n$ git config --list --show-origin   # everything, plus which file it came from\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Three levels of settings","md":"Git reads settings from three places, and the most specific one wins:\n\n| Level | Flag | Stored in | Applies to |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| System | `--system` | `/etc/gitconfig` | every user on the machine |\n| Global | `--global` | `~/.gitconfig` | every repository of *your* user |\n| Local | (no flag) | `.git/config` inside a repository | only that repository |\n\nSo you can set your personal email globally, and override it inside your work repositories:\n\n```term\n$ cd ~/work/payroll\n$ git config user.email \"kofi@company.com\"   # local: only this repo\n```\n\nThe settings are plain text files. `~/.gitconfig` looks like this:\n\n```text\n[user]\n\tname = Kofi Mensah\n\temail = kofi@example.com\n[init]\n\tdefaultBranch = main\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Your name badge","md":"Configuring git is like filling in the name badge you wear at a conference. You only do it once at the door, but it is on display in every conversation you have afterwards.\n\nThe global/local levels are like wearing your personal badge everywhere, but swapping to a company badge while you are at the company's stand."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What teams usually standardise","md":"Teams often add a few more global settings in their onboarding guide:\n\n```term\n$ git config --global pull.rebase true          # keep history linear when pulling\n$ git config --global push.autoSetupRemote true # first push of a branch sets its upstream\n$ git config --global fetch.prune true          # forget branches deleted on the server\n$ git config --global alias.st status           # shortcuts\n```\n\nYou will learn what each of these means later in the path. The point now: **git is configurable, and teams share good defaults.** When you join a team, ask for their recommended git settings."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Configuration gotchas","md":"**Typos in the key.** `git config --global user.mail` (missing `e`) silently creates a useless setting. Check with `git config --list`.\n\n**Forgetting `--global`.** Without it, the setting applies only to the repository you are standing in, and only if you are inside one.\n\n**Using the wrong email.** If the email does not match one on your GitHub account, your commits show up as an unknown grey avatar.\n\n**Quotes.** Names with spaces need quotes: `git config --global user.name \"Ada Lovelace\"`. Without them, git keeps only \"Ada\"."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Introduce yourself to git","md":"You are on a brand-new laptop. Git is installed but knows nothing about you: try `git config --list` and you will see it is almost empty.\n\nSet your identity and sensible defaults, then confirm everything is in place. Use your own name or a made-up one, but make the email look like an email."}},"basecamp/git-vs-github":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Just put it on Git\"","md":"On her first day at a fintech startup, Ama was told: \"Clone the repo from GitHub, make a branch, and open a PR when you're done.\" She nodded and then searched for \"how to download Git from GitHub.\"\n\nThat search reveals a very common confusion. Git and GitHub are two different things that work together, like **email** and **Gmail**:\n\n- **Email** is a protocol: a way of sending messages that many programs understand.\n- **Gmail** is one company's service built around email, with a website, search, spam filters and more.\n\nLikewise:\n\n- **Git** is a program that runs on *your* computer and tracks the history of a project folder. It is free, open source and works completely offline.\n- **GitHub** is a website (owned by Microsoft) that stores git repositories online and adds collaboration features: pull requests, code review, issues, automation (Actions), and a social network for code.\n\nAma's instructions really meant: *use git on your laptop to copy the project from GitHub, make your changes with git, then use GitHub's website to propose them.*"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Who does what","md":"```diagram\ngit-vs-github\n```\n\n| | Git | GitHub |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is it? | A command-line program | A website and cloud service |\n| Where does it run? | Your computer | Microsoft's servers |\n| Needs internet? | No | Yes |\n| Made by | Linus Torvalds (2005) and open-source contributors | GitHub Inc., founded 2008, Microsoft since 2018 |\n| Main jobs | Commits, branches, merges, history | Hosting repos, pull requests, reviews, issues, Actions, Pages |\n| Alternatives | Mercurial, SVN (older) | GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure Repos, Gitea |\n\nGit came first. Linus Torvalds wrote it in 2005 to manage the Linux kernel, a project with thousands of contributors. Its design goals were speed, integrity (no silent corruption), and letting everyone work independently with a full copy of the history."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Notebook and library","md":"Git is your **notebook**. You write in it at home, you can flip back through every page, and nobody else needs to be involved.\n\nGitHub is the **library** where you place a copy of your notebook so others can read it, make their own copies, suggest changes on sticky notes, and discuss them. The library also has noticeboards (issues), a review desk (pull requests) and a robot that checks every suggestion (Actions).\n\nYou can keep a notebook without ever going to a library. But to work with a team, you need a shared place, and GitHub is the most popular one."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Talking to each","md":"You will use two command-line tools:\n\n```term\n$ git --version\ngit version 2.47.0\n$ gh --version\ngh version 2.63.0\n```\n\n- `git` does everything local: history, commits, branches, merges. It also moves commits to and from servers (`git clone`, `git push`, `git pull`).\n- `gh` is GitHub's official command-line tool. It does GitHub things without opening the browser: create repositories, open pull requests, view issues.\n\n```term\n$ gh auth status                 # am I logged in to GitHub?\n$ gh repo view octo-org/handbook # read a repository's description and README\n$ git clone https://github.com/octo-org/handbook.git   # copy it to my machine\n```\n\nNotice that the URL in `git clone` is just an address. Git does not care that it is GitHub; the same command works with GitLab or your company's own server."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Where the line falls at work","md":"On a typical team day:\n\n| Task | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Write code, commit, switch branches | git, on your laptop |\n| Get teammates' latest work | `git pull`, from GitHub |\n| Share your branch | `git push`, to GitHub |\n| Propose merging your branch | GitHub pull request (website or `gh pr create`) |\n| Review a colleague's change | GitHub's review screen |\n| Run tests on every change | GitHub Actions |\n| Track bugs and tasks | GitHub Issues (or Jira, Linear…) |\n\nSome companies use GitLab or Bitbucket instead. Everything you learn about git transfers 100%. The GitHub-specific parts (PRs, Actions) have close equivalents everywhere: GitLab calls a pull request a \"merge request.\""},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Mix-ups to avoid","md":"**\"I'll download git from GitHub.\"** Install git from git-scm.com (or your OS package manager). GitHub is not required to use git.\n\n**\"My code is on Git.\"** People say this, but strictly your code is in a git *repository*, which may *also* be on GitHub.\n\n**\"If GitHub is down, I can't work.\"** You can keep committing locally. You only need GitHub to share.\n\n**\"GitHub Desktop is git.\"** It is a graphical app that runs git commands for you. Helpful, but learn the commands first: every tool, tutorial and error message speaks git."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Meet both tools","md":"Your laptop has git installed and you are logged in to a (simulated) GitHub account named `you`. An organisation called `octo-org` has published its team handbook as a public repository.\n\nUse both tools: ask git and gh who they are, read the repository on GitHub, then make your own local copy with git. Open the **GitHub** panel to see the website side."}},"basecamp/terminal-survival":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Why developers live in a text window","md":"Nadia, a designer moving into front-end development, asked her mentor why everyone at the company typed commands into a black window when there were perfectly good apps with buttons.\n\nHer mentor opened a terminal and typed one line:\n\n```term\n$ git log --author=\"Nadia\" --since=\"1 month ago\" --oneline\n```\n\nIt listed every change Nadia had made in the last month, across a project with 40,000 files, in under a second. \"Find me the button for that,\" he said.\n\nButtons are great for things you do the same way every time. The terminal is better when you want to **say exactly what you mean**: combine options, repeat a command, paste it into a chat so a colleague can run the identical thing, or put it into an automated script. Git was born on the command line, and every git tool with buttons is running these same commands underneath.\n\nYou do not need to become a terminal expert. You need about ten commands. This lesson gives you all of them."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Giving directions in a building","md":"Using a terminal is like moving around a large building while giving instructions out loud.\n\n- You are always **standing in one room**: the *current directory*. `pwd` (\"print working directory\") answers \"where am I?\"\n- `ls` (\"list\") is looking around the room: what files and doors are here?\n- `cd hallway` (\"change directory\") walks through a door into the next room. `cd ..` steps back out to the room you came from. `cd ~` teleports you to your own office: your *home directory*.\n- `mkdir storage` builds a new room. `touch notes.txt` puts an empty sheet of paper on the desk. `rm notes.txt` shreds it, and there is no recycling bin, so be sure.\n\nA **path** is a set of directions. `projects/shop/README.md` means \"through the projects door, then the shop door, then the README.md sheet.\" Directions that start with `/` start from the building's front entrance (the *root*), no matter where you stand."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"The survival kit","md":"Every command follows the same pattern: **name**, then **options** (start with `-`), then **arguments** (what to act on).\n\n```term\n$ ls -a projects\n#  ^   ^  ^ argument: which folder\n#  |   option: -a shows hidden files too\n#  command name\n```\n\n| Command | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pwd` | Show the folder you are in | `pwd` |\n| `ls` | List files; `-a` includes hidden ones (names starting with `.`) | `ls -a` |\n| `cd <dir>` | Move into a folder; `cd ..` goes up, `cd ~` goes home | `cd projects` |\n| `mkdir <dir>` | Make a folder; `-p` makes parent folders too | `mkdir -p code/demo` |\n| `touch <file>` | Create an empty file | `touch notes.txt` |\n| `echo \"text\" > file` | Write text into a file, **replacing** what was there | `echo \"hi\" > a.txt` |\n| `echo \"text\" >> file` | **Append** a line to the end of a file | `echo \"more\" >> a.txt` |\n| `cat <file>` | Print a file | `cat a.txt` |\n| `mv <a> <b>` | Move or rename | `mv a.txt b.txt` |\n| `cp <a> <b>` | Copy | `cp b.txt c.txt` |\n| `rm <file>` | Delete a file; `-r` for folders | `rm c.txt` |\n| `clear` | Clear the screen | `clear` |\n\nTwo keyboard habits save hours:\n\n- Press {kbd:Tab} to auto-complete file and folder names. Type `cd pro` then {kbd:Tab}.\n- Press {kbd:↑} to bring back your previous command, then edit it.\n\nIn this simulator you also have `edit <file>` (aliases `nano`, `code`), which opens a file in an editor panel. In real life you would open the file in VS Code or another editor."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Files, folders and hidden things","md":"Your files form a tree. Here is the home folder you will work in throughout this path:\n\n```term\n/home/dev            ← your home, also written ~\n├── projects/\n│   └── shop/\n│       ├── .git/    ← hidden: git's database (you'll meet it soon)\n│       ├── index.html\n│       └── README.md\n└── notes.txt\n```\n\nA few rules worth burning in:\n\n- Names starting with a dot (`.git`, `.gitignore`, `.env`) are **hidden**. `ls` skips them; `ls -a` shows them. Git uses dot-files a lot.\n- `.` means \"this folder\" and `..` means \"the parent folder\". `git add .` means \"add everything in this folder\".\n- Spaces in names cause pain: `my file.txt` needs quotes (`cat \"my file.txt\"`). Developers prefer `my-file.txt`.\n- **`>` replaces, `>>` appends.** Mixing them up is the most common way beginners wipe out a file."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"The terminal at work","md":"On a real team, the terminal is how you:\n\n- run the project (`npm run dev`, `python manage.py runserver`),\n- run tests before you push (`npm test`),\n- use git and GitHub's `gh` tool,\n- connect to servers (`ssh deploy@server`),\n- follow setup instructions in a README, which are almost always written as commands to paste.\n\nBeing able to read a command and predict what it will do is a core professional skill. When a teammate pastes a command in chat, read it before you run it, especially anything with `rm`, `--force` or `sudo`.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> On Windows, install **Git for Windows**, which includes \"Git Bash\", a terminal where every command in this path works. On macOS use the Terminal app; on Linux, any terminal."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Terminal traps","md":"**Running a command in the wrong folder.** Many surprises come from being one folder off. When in doubt, `pwd` and `ls` first.\n\n**`>` when you meant `>>`.** `echo \"new line\" > notes.txt` deletes everything else in notes.txt.\n\n**`rm` is forever.** There is no undo in the terminal. (Git can save you, but only for files it was tracking. You will see how.)\n\n**Fear of the error message.** Errors are information. Read the first line and the last line; they usually say exactly what went wrong.\n\n**Typing commands from images.** Copying a command with a \"smart quote\" (`“` instead of `\"`) breaks it. Type quotes yourself."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find your way around","md":"Your home folder already has a little content. Work through the tasks to practise every survival command. The **Files** panel shows what is on disk as you go.\n\nIf you get lost, `pwd` tells you where you are and `cd ~` takes you home."}},"basecamp/why-version-control":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The Friday the website broke","md":"Farhan had been at his first developer job for three weeks. On Friday afternoon he made a \"small change\" to the checkout page of a busy online shop, uploaded it to the server, and went home.\n\nBy 8 pm the shop had taken zero orders. The checkout button did nothing.\n\nHis manager called. \"What did you change?\" Farhan was not sure. He had edited four files that week. He had a folder on his desktop called `shop_backup_tuesday`, but he had kept working after Tuesday, and he could not remember which of his edits came before or after it. Undoing everything would also throw away three days of good work.\n\nThey fixed it at 1 am by comparing files line by line.\n\nOn Monday the team lead sat with him for ten minutes:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -3\n9fba052 Change checkout button handler\n98969a4 Add discount code field\n085ad89 Fix typo on product page\n$ git revert 9fba052\n[main 6546696] Revert \"Change checkout button handler\"\n```\n\nTwo commands. The history showed exactly which change happened when, and the broken change was undone without touching the good ones. That is what version control gives you: **a precise, trustworthy memory of every change**, and the power to move through it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> You will learn every command above in this path. For now, notice the shape of the fix: find the change, undo only that change."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Save points, but smarter","md":"Think of a long video game. Before the boss fight you **save**. If the fight goes badly, you **load** that save and try again. Without save points, one mistake costs you hours.\n\nVersion control is a save system for your project, with a few superpowers no game has:\n\n- **Every save has a note.** \"Add discount code field\" tells you *why* this save exists.\n- **You can see the difference between any two saves**, line by line.\n- **You can undo one old change** while keeping everything after it, like removing one bad move from the middle of a game.\n- **Several people can play at once** on their own copies and later combine their progress.\n- **Nothing is overwritten.** Saving again never destroys an earlier save.\n\nA second picture: a scientist's lab notebook. Every experiment gets a dated entry: what was done, by whom, and why. Nobody tears pages out. When something surprising happens months later, the notebook explains it. Git is that notebook for files, and it fills itself in with exact detail."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a version control system records","md":"Without version control, people invent their own system, and it always decays into this:\n\n```diagram\nvcs-chaos\n```\n\nA version control system (VCS) replaces the copies with **one folder and a history**. Each entry in the history (git calls it a **commit**) records:\n\n| Question | What git stores |\n|---|---|\n| What changed? | A full snapshot of every tracked file at that moment |\n| Who changed it? | The author's name and email |\n| When? | A timestamp |\n| Why? | A message written by the author |\n| What came before? | A link to the previous commit |\n\nThat last row matters most. Because each commit points at the one before it, the commits form a **chain**: the history of the project. Everything else you will learn (branches, merges, GitHub, pull requests) is a way of creating, moving along or combining these chains.\n\nGit is a **distributed** VCS: every person's copy of a project holds the *complete* history, not just the latest files. You can browse history and commit on a plane with no internet. Servers like GitHub are just one more copy that everyone agrees to share."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How real teams depend on it","md":"Version control is not optional in professional software. Nearly every company, from a two-person agency to Google, keeps its code in a VCS, and most use git. Here is what that looks like day to day:\n\n- **Parallel work.** Five developers change the same app at once, each on their own branch, and git combines their work.\n- **Code review.** Before a change goes live, a teammate reads exactly what changed (a \"diff\") and approves it. On GitHub this is a pull request.\n- **Deployments.** The live website is built from a specific commit. If a release breaks, the team redeploys the previous commit in minutes.\n- **Archaeology.** Six months later someone asks \"why does the timeout say 47 seconds?\" `git blame` shows the commit, the author and the message that explains it.\n- **Accountability without blame.** Every change has a name on it, which makes it easy to ask the right person a question.\n\nIt is not only for code. Writers keep books in git, scientists track analysis scripts and data notebooks, designers version design tokens, governments publish laws on GitHub, and system administrators version their server configuration."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Myths that trip up beginners","md":"**\"Git is a backup.\"** Not by itself. A repository on your laptop dies with your laptop. Git becomes a backup when you **push** to another machine, such as GitHub.\n\n**\"Git and GitHub are the same thing.\"** Git is a program on your computer. GitHub is a website that hosts git repositories and adds collaboration features. You will untangle these properly two lessons from now.\n\n**\"Version control is for big teams.\"** A solo developer benefits just as much: undo, history and safe experiments are useful even if nobody else ever sees the project.\n\n**\"I'll commit when it's finished.\"** The value comes from *small, frequent* commits. A history with one giant commit called \"stuff\" is almost as useless as no history.\n\n**\"Git stores only the differences.\"** Conceptually git stores full **snapshots**; it is just very clever about not storing the same content twice. Thinking in snapshots will make later topics much easier.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Version control will feel like extra steps for about a week. After that, working without it feels like writing without an undo key."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tour a project's memory","md":"You have just joined a small bakery's website team. The project in `~/bakery` already uses git. Before learning any mechanics, see what a history can tell you.\n\nYou will use three commands: `git log` (list the history), `git show <id>` (look at one commit in detail) and `cat` (print a file). Every commit has an id like `a1b2c3d`; you only need the first 7 characters.\n\nThe panel on the right shows the same history as a graph. Watch it as you go."}},"first-commits/commits":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"fixed stuff\"","md":"When Priya joined a payments team, she was asked to find out why refunds sometimes took two days. She opened the history of the refunds module:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -- refunds/\nc41e9a2 fixed stuff\n88b0d1e more changes\n5aa2f03 wip\n1d9e774 update\n```\n\nFour commits, four useless messages. She had to open each one and read every line to understand what happened.\n\nIn the payments service next door, the history read like a story:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -- payouts/\ne8f0c11 Retry failed payouts after 15 minutes instead of 24 hours\n7b2d5a9 Log the bank's error code when a payout is rejected\n40c8e7e Add payout status to the merchant dashboard\n```\n\nShe found the answer there in under a minute. A commit message is a note to your future self and your teammates. It costs ten seconds to write well and saves hours later."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"git commit","md":"```term\n$ git commit -m \"Add packing list for Japan\"     # commit what is staged, with a message\n[main 3f2a9c1] Add packing list for Japan\n 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)\n create mode 100644 japan/packing.md\n```\n\nThe output tells you the branch (`main`), the new commit's short id (`3f2a9c1`), its message, and a summary of what changed.\n\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `git commit -m \"msg\"` | Commit the staging area with a one-line message |\n| `git commit -m \"Title\" -m \"Body\"` | Two `-m` options: a title line, then a longer explanation |\n| `git commit` | Opens your editor to write the message (save and close to commit) |\n| `git commit -am \"msg\"` | Stage every **tracked** modified file, then commit. New files are not included. |\n| `git commit --amend` | Replace the last commit. You will master this in the Time Travel realm. |\n\nIf nothing is staged, git refuses:\n\n```term\n$ git commit -m \"Update\"\nOn branch main\nChanges not staged for commit:\n\tmodified:   index.html\n\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a commit is","md":"```diagram\ncommit-anatomy\n```\n\nA commit is a small record that points at a full snapshot of the staging area, plus metadata: author, committer, timestamps, the message, and the id of its **parent** commit. Its id is the SHA-1 hash of all of that.\n\nBecause each commit names its parent, commits form a chain going back to the very first commit (the *root commit*, which has no parent):\n\n```replay\ntitle: Commits link to their parents\nshow: graph\nsetup: init\nstep: commit \"Create blog skeleton\" | The root commit has no parent.\nstep: commit \"Add Lisbon post\" | The second commit records the first as its parent.\nstep: commit \"Add Porto post\" | And so on. The label main always points at the newest commit on the branch.\n```\n\nCommits are **immutable**. You can never change a commit; \"editing\" one (amend, rebase) really creates a new commit with a new id and moves the branch label to it."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Entries in a ship's log","md":"A captain's log has one entry per watch: the time, who wrote it, what happened, and it follows the previous entry. Nobody rips pages out. When something goes wrong, the investigators read the log in order.\n\nGood log entries say what happened and why: *\"Changed course to 270° to avoid a storm cell.\"* Bad entries say *\"stuff\"*. A commit message is a log entry for your project."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Writing messages your team will thank you for","md":"The widely used convention:\n\n```text\nShort summary in the imperative, under ~50 characters\n\nOptional body after a blank line, wrapped at ~72 characters.\nExplain WHY the change was needed and anything surprising about\nthe approach. The diff already shows WHAT changed.\n\nFixes #142\n```\n\n- **Imperative mood**: \"Add search box\", not \"Added search box\" or \"Adds search box\". Read it as \"If applied, this commit will *add search box*.\"\n- **One logical change per commit.** If your message needs the word \"and\", consider two commits.\n- **Reference issues** (\"Fixes #142\") so GitHub links them.\n- Many teams also use **Conventional Commits**: `feat: add search box`, `fix(auth): handle expired tokens`, `docs: explain setup`. You will meet them in the Team Workflows realm.\n\n| Weak message | Strong message |\n|---|---|\n| fixed bug | Fix crash when a cart is empty at checkout |\n| changes | Rename \"Buy\" button to \"Add to cart\" |\n| wip | Add draft of the pricing page (not linked yet) |\n| update README | Document how to run the tests locally |"},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Commit pitfalls","md":"**Stuck in the editor.** `git commit` without `-m` opens an editor. If that editor is Vim, you may feel trapped: press {kbd:Esc}, type `:wq` and press {kbd:Enter} to save and quit. Or configure a friendlier editor (`core.editor`). In this simulator the editor is a panel with Save and Cancel buttons.\n\n**`-am` does not add new files.** `git commit -am` only stages files git already tracks.\n\n**Committing secrets.** Passwords and API keys in a commit stay in history forever, even if a later commit deletes them. Check `git status` and `git diff --staged` before committing.\n\n**Giant commits.** A commit that touches 40 files for 5 different reasons is almost impossible to review or revert. Commit small and often."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Commit like a professional","md":"You will make several commits for the travel blog, including one with a title and a body, and one written in the editor. Aim for clear, imperative messages: the lab checks the shape of your messages."}},"first-commits/diff-log":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"What did I just change?\"","md":"Tomás has been editing a configuration file for an hour, trying to make the app connect to a new database. It finally works, but he changed a dozen things along the way and is no longer sure which of them mattered.\n\n```term\n$ git diff config.yml\n-  timeout: 5\n+  timeout: 30\n-  host: db-old.internal\n+  host: db-new.internal\n+  ssl: true\n```\n\nThree changes. He now knows exactly what to commit, and he spotted that the timeout change was only an experiment he should undo.\n\nBeing able to answer \"what changed?\" at any moment, between any two points in history, is one of git's superpowers. This lesson covers the three commands that answer it: `git diff`, `git log` and `git show`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Which diff compares what","md":"Each `git diff` form compares two of the three areas:\n\n| Command | Compares | Answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `git diff` | staging area → working directory | What have I changed but not staged? |\n| `git diff --staged` | last commit → staging area | What will my next commit contain? |\n| `git diff HEAD` | last commit → working directory | Everything I changed since the last commit |\n| `git diff A B` | commit A → commit B | What changed between two points in history |\n\nReading a diff:\n\n```term\ndiff --git a/menu.txt b/menu.txt      ← which file\nindex 3b18e51..a5c1f2d 100644          ← blob ids before..after\n--- a/menu.txt                         ← \"before\" side\n+++ b/menu.txt                         ← \"after\" side\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@                         ← hunk: lines 1-3 before, lines 1-3 after\n Soup: tomato                           ← unchanged (context)\n-Main: pasta                            ← removed\n+Main: risotto                          ← added\n Dessert: cake\n```\n\nGit shows a modified line as one removal and one addition. Three lines of unchanged context surround each change so you can see where it is."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"log and show","md":"```term\n$ git log                         # full history, newest first\n$ git log --oneline               # one line per commit\n$ git log --oneline --graph --all # history of every branch as a graph\n$ git log -n 3                    # only the last three commits\n$ git log --stat                  # which files each commit touched\n$ git log -p README.md            # every change ever made to one file, with diffs\n$ git log --author=\"Tomás\"        # filter by author\n$ git log --grep=\"timeout\"        # filter by message text\n$ git show                        # the latest commit: metadata + diff\n$ git show 3f2a9c1                # a specific commit\n$ git show HEAD~1:config.yml      # a file exactly as it was one commit ago\n```\n\n`HEAD` means \"the commit you are on now\". `HEAD~1` is its parent, `HEAD~2` the grandparent, and so on. You can use these anywhere git expects a commit."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Track changes, but better","md":"If you have used \"Track changes\" in a word processor, a diff will feel familiar: deletions marked in red, additions in green. The difference is that git can show you the tracked changes between **any two moments** in the project's life, for every file at once, and nobody has to remember to switch tracking on.\n\n`git log` is the table of contents of that history; `git show` opens one chapter."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Diffs at work","md":"- **Before committing**: `git diff --staged` is your last check. Professionals read their own diff before anyone else does.\n- **Code review**: a pull request on GitHub is essentially a diff with comments. Learning to read diffs fluently makes you a better reviewer.\n- **Debugging**: \"it worked yesterday\" becomes `git log --since=yesterday` followed by `git show` on each suspect.\n- **Stand-ups**: `git log --author=me --since=\"1 day ago\" --oneline` reminds you what you did."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Diff confusion","md":"**\"`git diff` shows nothing, but I changed files!\"** Your changes are probably staged. Plain `git diff` only shows unstaged changes; use `git diff --staged`.\n\n**New files do not appear in `git diff`.** Untracked files are not compared at all until you `git add` them; then they appear in `git diff --staged`.\n\n**The pager.** In a real terminal, long output opens in a pager (`less`). Press {kbd:q} to quit, {kbd:Space} for the next page, {kbd:/} to search.\n\n**Reading the wrong direction.** `git diff A B` shows how to get from A to B. Swap them and every `+` becomes `-`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Investigate the menu","md":"A restaurant's menu project has some history and some unfinished edits. Use `diff`, `log` and `show` to investigate, then commit only after you have checked exactly what you are committing."}},"first-commits/first-commits-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Ship version one","md":"**The scenario.** A local football club asked you for a simple website. You have been working in a plain folder, `~/club-site`, without version control (oops). Today you put it under git properly and ship version one.\n\nThe folder contains real source files, but also a downloaded library folder, a log file, a secrets file and some editor junk. The club's coach also asked for one last-minute change.\n\nYou will need everything from this realm:\n\n| Skill | Commands |\n|---|---|\n| Start tracking | `git init` |\n| Keep junk out | `.gitignore`, `git status --ignored` |\n| Build focused commits | `git add <file>`, `git status`, `git diff --staged` |\n| Write good messages | `git commit -m \"Imperative summary\"` |\n| Inspect | `git log --oneline`, `git show` |\n| Fix file mistakes | `git restore`, `git rm --cached`, `git mv` |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The boss checks the final state of the repository, not the exact commands you type. Plan the commits before you make them."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What First Commits gave you","md":"- `git init` turns a folder into a repository by creating `.git`, git's database.\n- Every file is **untracked**, **modified**, **staged** or **unmodified**. `git add` stages; `git commit` records the staging area as a snapshot.\n- A commit stores a full snapshot plus author, time, message and parent. Ids are SHA-1 hashes; commits are immutable.\n- `git diff` (unstaged), `git diff --staged` (staged), `git log`, `git show` let you inspect any change.\n- `.gitignore` keeps generated, personal and secret files out. It only affects untracked files.\n- `git rm`, `git mv`, `git restore` handle deletions, renames and undoing edits.\n\nNext realm: Time Travel. You will move through history and undo almost anything."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The club website goes live","md":""}},"first-commits/gitignore":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The 400 MB commit","md":"On his first pull request, Diego's diff showed 18,437 changed files. Reviewers' browsers froze. He had committed the `node_modules/` folder (all the downloaded libraries, which anyone can recreate with `npm install`), a `.DS_Store` file from his Mac, the `dist/` build output, and, worst of all, a `.env` file containing the production database password.\n\nThe libraries and build output were just noise. The password was a security incident: it had to be changed immediately, because once it is pushed to GitHub you must assume someone has seen it.\n\nAll of this is prevented by one small text file named `.gitignore`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What belongs in a repository","md":"**Commit** what people need to rebuild and understand the project:\n\n- source code, tests, documentation,\n- configuration that is the same for everyone (with **no secrets**),\n- the dependency *list* (`package.json`, `requirements.txt`), not the dependencies themselves.\n\n**Ignore** anything that is generated, personal, huge or secret:\n\n| Kind | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Dependencies | `node_modules/`, `vendor/`, `.venv/` |\n| Build output | `dist/`, `build/`, `*.class`, `*.o` |\n| Logs and temp files | `*.log`, `tmp/`, `*.swp` |\n| OS and editor junk | `.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db`, `.idea/`, `.vscode/` (sometimes) |\n| Secrets | `.env`, `*.pem`, `credentials.json` |\n\n`.gitignore` only affects **untracked** files. If a file is already committed, adding it to `.gitignore` does nothing until you stop tracking it with `git rm --cached`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Pattern syntax","md":"`.gitignore` lists one pattern per line:\n\n```text\n# Comments start with a hash\nnode_modules/      # a trailing slash matches folders only\n*.log              # any file ending in .log, in any folder\n/build             # a leading slash anchors to the repository root only\n.env               # an exact name, anywhere\n!keep.log          # \"!\" re-includes something an earlier pattern excluded\ndocs/**/*.pdf      # ** matches any number of folders\n```\n\nThen commit the `.gitignore` itself, so the whole team shares the rules:\n\n```term\n$ echo \"node_modules/\" >> .gitignore\n$ git add .gitignore\n$ git commit -m \"Ignore installed dependencies\"\n```\n\nUseful checks:\n\n```term\n$ git status --ignored          # also list what is being ignored\n$ git rm --cached .env          # stop tracking a file that was committed by mistake (keeps it on disk)\n$ git add -f special.log        # force-add one ignored file (rarely needed)\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> GitHub keeps ready-made templates at github.com/github/gitignore (Node, Python, Java…), and offers to add one when you create a repository."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The bouncer's list","md":"`.gitignore` is the list held by the bouncer at the door of your repository. Anyone on the list is turned away from `git add`, quietly, without an argument. But the list only applies at the door: someone already inside the club (a file already tracked) is not thrown out just because their name is added to the list later. You have to escort them out yourself with `git rm --cached`."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Ignore rules on real projects","md":"- Add `.gitignore` in the **very first commit**, before anyone runs `git add .`.\n- Teams commit an **example** config with fake values (`.env.example`) and ignore the real one (`.env`).\n- Personal junk from your own tools (editor folders, OS files) can go in a **global** ignore file so you do not have to add it to every project: `git config --global core.excludesFile ~/.gitignore_global`.\n- When a secret is committed and pushed, removing it in a new commit is **not enough**. It remains in history. Rotate (change) the secret immediately, then consider rewriting history. The Hero Trials realm covers this."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Ignore gotchas","md":"**Adding a tracked file to `.gitignore` and expecting it to vanish.** It does not. Use `git rm --cached <file>` and commit.\n\n**Ignoring `.gitignore`.** Commit it. It is part of the project.\n\n**`*.log` vs `/logs`.** `*.log` matches files anywhere; `/logs` matches only a top-level `logs` entry; `logs/` matches any folder named logs.\n\n**Negation inside an ignored folder.** `node_modules/` followed by `!node_modules/keep.js` does not work: git does not even look inside an ignored folder. Ignore the folder's contents (`node_modules/*`) if you need exceptions."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Keep the junk out","md":"A web project has collected dependencies, logs, build output, an editor folder and a secrets file. Someone even committed a log file already. Write a `.gitignore` that keeps everything except the source out, fix the file that was tracked by mistake, and commit.\n\nUse `edit .gitignore` to write the file comfortably, or build it line by line with `echo \"pattern\" >> .gitignore`."}},"first-commits/init":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"A folder becomes a repository","md":"Lena runs a small cookbook blog and writes her recipes as text files in a folder called `recipes`. She keeps losing good versions of recipes when she \"improves\" them. A developer friend tells her: \"Run `git init` in that folder.\"\n\n```term\n$ cd recipes\n$ git init\nInitialized empty Git repository in /home/lena/recipes/.git/\n```\n\nNothing visible changed. Her recipe files are exactly where they were. But the folder now has a hidden subfolder, `.git`, and that changes everything: the folder is now a **repository**, and git can record its history.\n\nNothing is tracked yet, though. `git init` only builds the empty filing cabinet. Lena still has to decide which files go into it, and when. That is the next lesson."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What git init actually creates","md":"A repository is two things living in one folder:\n\n- **Your files** (the *working directory*): the recipes, code or documents you edit normally.\n- **The `.git` folder**: git's private database holding every commit, branch and setting.\n\n```term\nrecipes/\n├── .git/          ← the repository's database. Never edit by hand.\n│   ├── HEAD       ← which branch you are on: \"ref: refs/heads/main\"\n│   ├── config     ← settings for this repository only\n│   ├── objects/   ← every version of every file, compressed\n│   ├── refs/      ← branches and tags (tiny files holding commit ids)\n│   └── hooks/     ← optional scripts git can run automatically\n├── pancakes.md\n└── soup.md\n```\n\nDelete `.git` and the folder is an ordinary folder again, with **all history gone**. Copy the whole folder, `.git` included, and you have copied the repository with its full history.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> One project, one repository. Run `git init` in the project's top folder, not in your home folder. A repository in `~` would try to track every file you own."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Installing a flight recorder","md":"Think of `git init` as installing a flight recorder (a \"black box\") in an aircraft. Installing it does not record anything yet and does not change how the plane flies. But from now on, the recorder is there, ready to capture exactly what happens.\n\nThe `.git` folder is the black box. You do not open it during the flight; you use the instruments (git commands) to read and write it."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Creating repositories","md":"```term\n$ mkdir travel-blog && cd travel-blog   # make a project folder and enter it\n$ git init                              # turn it into a repository\nInitialized empty Git repository in /home/dev/travel-blog/.git/\n$ ls -a                                 # .git is hidden; -a shows it\n.  ..  .git\n$ git status                            # ask git what it sees\nOn branch main\n\nNo commits yet\n\nnothing to commit (create/copy files and use \"git add\" to track)\n```\n\nUseful variations:\n\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `git init` | Repository in the current folder |\n| `git init my-app` | Creates `my-app/` and a repository inside it |\n| `git init -b main` | Choose the first branch name for this repository |\n| `git status` | Inside a repo: what changed. Outside one: `fatal: not a git repository` |\n\nRunning `git init` twice is harmless: git replies `Reinitialized existing Git repository` and touches nothing."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Peeking at .git","md":"You never need to edit `.git` by hand, but looking inside once removes the mystery:\n\n```term\n$ cat .git/HEAD\nref: refs/heads/main\n$ ls .git\nconfig  description  HEAD  hooks  info  objects  refs\n```\n\n`HEAD` contains a single line saying \"the current branch is main.\" The branch `main` does not have a file in `refs/heads/` yet, because a branch is just a pointer to a commit and there are no commits. That is also why `git status` says \"No commits yet.\"\n\nIn this simulator you can `ls .git` and `cat .git/HEAD` too. Later, in the Power Tools realm, you will open the objects themselves."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Init mistakes","md":"**Initialising in the wrong folder.** If `git status` lists hundreds of files you never meant to track, you probably ran `git init` in your home folder. Fix: `rm -rf .git` in *that* folder (carefully: check `pwd` first).\n\n**Nesting repositories.** Running `git init` inside a folder that is already part of a repository creates a repository inside a repository. The outer one then sees the inner folder as a strange, untrackable thing. Check with `git status` before running `git init`.\n\n**Expecting init to save files.** After `git init`, nothing is tracked. Your files are not \"in git\" until you add and commit them.\n\n**Deleting `.git` to \"start fresh\".** That deletes the entire history. It is occasionally right, but never do it casually."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Start the travel blog","md":"You are starting a travel blog. Create the project folder, turn it into a repository, and look at what git created. Watch the **Files** and **Three areas** panels: the folder is a repository now, but the repository is still empty."}},"first-commits/rm-mv-restore":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The deleted stylesheet","md":"Ravi was tidying up a project and deleted `old-styles.css`. Ten minutes later the site looked broken: one page still used it. Before git, this meant digging through backups. Ravi typed one command:\n\n```term\n$ git restore old-styles.css\n```\n\nThe file was back, exactly as it was in the last commit.\n\nRemoving, renaming and restoring files are everyday tasks. Git can do all of them, and it helps to know how each one looks from git's point of view."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Deletes and renames are changes too","md":"Git compares the working directory with the staging area and the last commit. So:\n\n- **Deleting** a tracked file is a change (\"deleted: old-styles.css\") that you stage and commit like any other.\n- **Renaming** a file looks like a deletion of the old name plus a new untracked file, until both halves are staged; then git recognises it as a **rename** because the content is the same.\n- **Restoring** copies a version from the staging area or a commit back over your working file.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Renaming a file, two ways\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add notes\" notes.txt\nstep: mv notes.txt journal.txt | A plain mv: git sees notes.txt deleted and journal.txt untracked.\nstep: git add -A | After staging both sides, git recognises a rename (same content, new name).\nstep: git commit -m \"Rename notes to journal\" | Committed. git mv would have done the mv and the staging in one step.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"rm, mv, restore","md":"```term\n$ git rm old.css                 # delete the file AND stage the deletion\n$ git rm --cached secrets.txt    # stop tracking, keep the file on disk\n$ git rm -r old-folder/          # remove a whole folder\n$ git mv draft.md post.md        # rename and stage in one step\n$ git restore app.js             # discard unstaged edits: back to the staged version\n$ git restore --staged app.js    # unstage (keep the edit in the working directory)\n$ git restore --source=HEAD~2 app.js   # bring back the version from two commits ago\n$ git restore .                  # discard every unstaged change (careful!)\n```\n\n| You want to… | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Throw away edits you have not staged | `git restore <file>` |\n| Take a file out of the staging area | `git restore --staged <file>` |\n| Undo a `git rm` that is not committed yet | `git restore --staged <file>` then `git restore <file>` |\n| Recover a file deleted in an earlier commit | `git restore --source=<commit-before-deletion> <file>` |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Older tutorials use `git checkout -- <file>` and `git reset HEAD <file>` for the same jobs. They still work. `git restore` (added in Git 2.23) does one thing and is clearer."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The archive and the draft","md":"Think of the last commit as a signed, archived copy of every document, and your working directory as drafts on your desk. `git restore file` says \"throw away my draft of this document and give me a fresh copy from the archive.\" It cannot fail to find the archived copy, but it **does** destroy your draft, so be sure the draft is worthless."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Restore with care","md":"**`git restore` destroys uncommitted edits.** There is no undo for discarding changes that were never committed or staged. If unsure, `git stash` first (you will meet it in the Branching realm) or copy the file.\n\n**`git rm` vs `rm`.** Plain `rm` deletes the file; you still need to stage the deletion (`git add -A` or `git rm`). `git rm` does both.\n\n**`git rm --cached` is what you want for secrets and junk**, not `git rm`, which would also delete your local copy.\n\n**Renaming case only** (`readme.md` to `README.md`) can confuse case-insensitive file systems (Windows, macOS). Use `git mv` for that."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Spring cleaning","md":"The blog has an outdated file, a badly named file, and a file you are about to break by accident. Use git's file commands, then commit the clean-up."}},"first-commits/three-areas":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two changes, one commit","md":"Sam is fixing a bug in an app's login page. While in there, he also starts redesigning the footer, but the footer is only half done when the bug fix is ready.\n\nSam wants to save the bug fix **now**, as one clean change the team can review and ship, and keep working on the footer without including it.\n\nMany version control systems would force Sam to save everything at once. Git gives him a middle step: he can **pick** which changes go into the next commit.\n\n```term\n$ git status\nChanges not staged for commit:\n\tmodified:   login.js\n\tmodified:   footer.css\n$ git add login.js\n$ git commit -m \"Fix login when the password contains a space\"\n```\n\nOnly `login.js` went into the commit. `footer.css` is still modified, waiting. This middle step is called the **staging area**, and it is the most important idea in this realm."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Working directory, staging area, repository","md":"```diagram\nthree-areas\n```\n\nGit keeps your project in three places at once:\n\n1. **Working directory**: the actual files on disk. You edit these.\n2. **Staging area** (also called **the index**): a draft of the next commit. `git add` copies a file's current content here.\n3. **Repository**: the permanent history inside `.git`. `git commit` turns the staging area into a new commit.\n\nEvery file you see is in one of these states:\n\n| State | Meaning | Shown by `git status` as |\n|---|---|---|\n| Untracked | Git has never been told about this file | `Untracked files:` (red) |\n| Modified | Tracked, changed since the last commit, not staged | `Changes not staged for commit:` (red) |\n| Staged | The change is in the staging area, ready to commit | `Changes to be committed:` (green) |\n| Unmodified | Same as the last commit | not listed at all |\n\nWatch a file travel through the areas:\n\n```replay\ntitle: One file through the three areas\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init\nstep: echo \"# Travel log\" > README.md | A new file appears in the working directory. Git sees it but does not track it: it is untracked.\nstep: git add README.md | git add copies the file's current content into the staging area. It is now staged as a new file.\nstep: git commit -m \"Start the travel log\" | git commit turns the staging area into a permanent snapshot. The repository now has its first commit.\nstep: echo \"Day 1: Lisbon\" >> README.md | Editing the file changes only the working directory. The staging area and repository still hold the old version, so the file is modified.\nstep: git add README.md | Staging again copies the new content into the staging area.\nstep: git commit -m \"Add the first day\" | A second commit. Each commit links to the one before it.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Packing a parcel","md":"Think of shipping a parcel:\n\n- Your **room** is the working directory: things are scattered everywhere, some finished, some not.\n- The open **box** on the table is the staging area. You choose what to put in it with `git add`.\n- **Sealing and posting** the box is `git commit`. Once posted, the parcel and its label (the commit message) are permanent.\n\nYou can put an item in the box and take it out again before sealing (`git restore --staged`). You can keep adding items over an afternoon. Nothing is shipped until you seal it. And if you change an item after putting it in the box, the box still holds the old version until you put the new one in."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"status and add","md":"```term\n$ git status            # the command you will run more than any other\n$ git status -s         # short form: two columns, staged | unstaged\n$ git add file.txt      # stage one file\n$ git add src/          # stage everything inside a folder\n$ git add .             # stage every change in this folder and below\n$ git add -A            # stage every change in the whole repository, including deletions\n```\n\nThe short format packs a lot into two letters:\n\n```term\n$ git status -s\nA  README.md      ← A in the left column: new file, staged\n M app.js         ← M in the right column: modified, not staged\nMM style.css      ← staged, then modified again\n?? notes.txt      ← untracked\n```\n\nLeft column = staging area compared to the last commit. Right column = working directory compared to the staging area.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Run `git status` before every `git add` and every `git commit`. It costs nothing and it is how professionals avoid committing the wrong thing."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Why teams love the staging area","md":"- **Focused commits.** A reviewer can understand \"Fix login when the password contains a space\" in thirty seconds. A commit called \"fix login + footer redesign + typo\" takes much longer and is harder to undo safely.\n- **Undo precision.** If the footer redesign causes a bug next month, the team can revert that commit without touching the login fix.\n- **Reviewing yourself.** Staging is a natural checkpoint: `git status` and `git diff --staged` show exactly what you are about to share. Many embarrassing commits (passwords, debug prints, huge files) are caught right here."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Staging surprises","md":"**\"I edited the file after `git add`, so the edit is staged.\"** No. `git add` copies the content *at that moment*. Edit again and you must add again. `git status` will show the file in both lists (`MM`).\n\n**`git add .` adds more than you think.** It stages everything under the current folder, including new files you forgot about. Check `git status` first. (A `.gitignore` file, coming soon, keeps junk out.)\n\n**Untracked files are invisible to commits.** A brand-new file is not included in any commit until you `git add` it, even with `git commit -a`.\n\n**Empty folders.** Git tracks files, not folders. An empty folder never shows up. People put a placeholder file like `.gitkeep` in folders they need to exist."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Pack the first parcel","md":"The travel blog has a few files already, but nothing has been committed. Practise moving files between the areas, and watch the **Three areas** panel after every command. You only need `git status`, `git add` and `git commit` for this lab."}},"time-travel/amend":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The forgotten file","md":"Leo committed his new search feature, looked at the result and winced:\n\n```term\n$ git commit -m \"Add serach to the product page\"\n[main 3e1f0a2] Add serach to the product page\n 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+)\n$ git status\nUntracked files:\n\tsearch.css\n```\n\nA typo in the message, and he had forgotten to add the stylesheet. He had not pushed yet, so he fixed both in one move:\n\n```term\n$ git add search.css\n$ git commit --amend -m \"Add search to the product page\"\n[main 9b77c40] Add search to the product page\n 2 files changed, 58 insertions(+)\n```\n\nThe history now shows one clean commit, as if the mistake never happened. Notice the id changed from `3e1f0a2` to `9b77c40`: amend does not edit a commit, it **replaces** it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Amend replaces the last commit","md":"```replay\ntitle: --amend swaps the tip commit for a new one\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add product page\"; commit \"Add serach\"\nstep: git commit --amend -m \"Add search\" | A new commit replaces the old tip. main now points to it. The old commit is no longer on any branch (it fades out), though the reflog still remembers it.\n```\n\n`git commit --amend` takes the **current staging area** and the parent of the last commit, and creates a new commit from them. So it can:\n\n- change the message (`-m \"new\"` or edit it in the editor),\n- add forgotten changes (stage them first),\n- remove a file from the last commit (`git rm --cached file`, then amend),\n- keep the message unchanged (`--no-edit`).\n\nThe old commit is not destroyed immediately. It just stops being part of the branch."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Amend recipes","md":"```term\n$ git commit --amend -m \"Better message\"   # fix only the message\n$ git add forgotten.css\n$ git commit --amend --no-edit             # add a file, keep the message\n$ git commit --amend --author=\"Leo Park <leo@example.com>\"   # fix the author\n$ git commit --amend                       # open the editor with the old message\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Reprinting the last page","md":"Amend is like reprinting the last page of a document before you have handed it in. The new page replaces the old one in the binder. But if you already gave a copy to a colleague, they hold the old page, and now the two binders disagree."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"The one rule","md":"**Amend only commits you have not pushed.** Once a commit is on a shared branch, teammates may have built on it. Replacing it means your history and theirs no longer match: your next push is rejected, and if you force it, you overwrite their view of history.\n\nOn your **own** feature branch, before anyone else uses it, amending is common and fine (you will then need `git push --force-with-lease`, covered in the Remotes realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Amend traps","md":"**Amending a pushed commit on `main`.** Don't. Make a new commit instead.\n\n**Accidentally amending.** You meant a new commit but typed `--amend`: the previous commit's content is merged into the new one. The reflog (later in this realm) can get the old commit back.\n\n**Expecting amend to touch older commits.** It only replaces the very last commit. For older ones you need interactive rebase (Rewriting History realm)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fix it before anyone sees","md":"You just made a commit with a typo in the message and a forgotten file, and then another small slip. Repair the last commit with amend, without creating extra commits."}},"time-travel/detached-head":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Did it work in March?\"","md":"A customer reported that the PDF export of invoices had been broken \"for a while.\" Hannah wanted to know whether it had ever worked. Instead of guessing, she went back in time:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline --until=\"2026-03-31\" -1\n7d3e9b2 Add invoice PDF export\n$ git switch --detach 7d3e9b2\nHEAD is now at 7d3e9b2 Add invoice PDF export\n```\n\nHer working directory now looked exactly like the project did that day. She ran the export: it worked. So the bug arrived later, which narrowed the search enormously. Then she returned to the present:\n\n```term\n$ git switch main\n```\n\nMoving to an old commit like this puts you in **detached HEAD** state. It sounds alarming. It is simply git's name for \"looking at a commit that is not the tip of a branch.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Where HEAD points","md":"```diagram\ndetached-head\n```\n\nNormally HEAD points at a **branch**, and the branch points at a commit. When you commit, the branch moves forward and HEAD comes along.\n\nWhen you check out a commit directly, HEAD points straight at **that commit**: it is *detached* from any branch. Everything still works: you can look, build, run tests, even commit. But new commits made here belong to no branch. Once you switch away, nothing points at them, and they will eventually be garbage-collected.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Detaching HEAD and coming back\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add invoices\"; commit \"Add PDF export\"; commit \"Redesign header\"; commit \"Add dark mode\"\nstep: git switch --detach HEAD~2 | HEAD now points directly at \"Add PDF export\". main has not moved.\nstep: commit \"Experiment: bigger font\" | A commit made here hangs off the old commit. No branch points at it.\nstep: git switch -c font-experiment | Creating a branch here gives the experiment a name, so it is safe.\nstep: git switch main | Back to the present. The experiment branch keeps its commit.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Visiting and returning","md":"```term\n$ git switch --detach <commit>      # visit a commit (modern)\n$ git checkout <commit>             # visit a commit (classic; prints the detached HEAD notice)\n$ git checkout v1.2.0               # visit a release tag\n$ git switch -                      # go back to where you were\n$ git switch main                   # return to a branch\n$ git switch -c rescue-branch       # keep commits made while detached\n```\n\nYou cannot switch while you have uncommitted changes that would be overwritten; git stops you with \"Your local changes would be overwritten by checkout.\" Commit or stash first."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A museum visit","md":"Detached HEAD is a museum visit to a past version of your project. You can walk around and look at everything. If you start building something in the museum, though, it is not part of any exhibition: when you leave, the cleaning staff will eventually clear it away. If you want to keep what you built, put a label on it (create a branch) before you leave."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"When developers travel back","md":"- **\"Did this ever work?\"**: check out an old release tag and test.\n- **Reproducing a customer's bug** on the exact version they run: `git checkout v2.3.1`.\n- **Comparing behaviour** before and after a change.\n- **Bisecting** (Power Tools realm) automates this: git detaches HEAD on commit after commit for you."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Detached HEAD traps","md":"**Committing while detached and then leaving.** Your commits are orphaned. If it happens, don't panic: `git reflog` (later in this realm) shows their ids, and `git branch rescue <id>` saves them.\n\n**Confusing `git checkout <file>` and `git checkout <commit>`.** The old `checkout` command does both jobs. `git switch` (branches/commits) and `git restore` (files) split them up, which is why modern docs prefer them.\n\n**Forgetting where you are.** Your prompt or `git status` says `HEAD detached at 7d3e9b2`. Check before you start real work."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Visit the past","md":"An invoicing app's history has a release tag and several later commits. Travel back, look around, make an experimental commit, save it with a branch, and return."}},"time-travel/history-detective":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Who changed the shipping fee?","md":"On Monday the support inbox of an online bookshop filled with complaints: shipping to Canada had jumped from $8 to $80. Somebody had added a zero. Nobody admitted it, nobody remembered it, and the change could have been made any time in the last two weeks among 140 commits.\n\nMei, the newest developer, found it in under two minutes:\n\n```term\n$ git log -S \"80\" --oneline -- shipping/rates.json\n4c1d0a7 Update rates for 2026\n$ git show 4c1d0a7 --stat\n shipping/rates.json | 2 +-\n$ git log -1 --format=\"%an, %ar\" 4c1d0a7\nJoel Kim, 6 days ago\n```\n\n`git log -S` finds commits that **added or removed** a piece of text. Combined with a path filter and `git show`, it pointed straight at the commit, its author and its date. Joel fixed the typo in a new commit five minutes later, and Mei earned a reputation as the person who can find anything.\n\nHistory is only valuable if you can search it. This lesson turns `git log` from a wall of text into a search engine."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Naming commits","md":"Before you can travel through history you need ways to **name** points in it:\n\n| Name | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `a1b2c3d` | A commit by (short) id. 7 characters are usually unique. |\n| `HEAD` | The commit you are on now |\n| `main`, `feature/x` | The commit a branch points at |\n| `HEAD~1`, `HEAD~3` | 1 or 3 generations back, following first parents |\n| `HEAD^` | The first parent (same as `HEAD~1`) |\n| `HEAD^2` | The **second** parent (only merge commits have one) |\n| `v1.0` | A tag |\n| `main..feature` | Commits on feature that are not on main |\n\n```replay\ntitle: HEAD~n walks back through parents\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"One\"; commit \"Two\"; commit \"Three\"; commit \"Four\"\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD | HEAD is the newest commit, \"Four\".\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD~1 | HEAD~1 is its parent, \"Three\".\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD~3 | HEAD~3 goes three generations back, to \"One\".\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Searching history","md":"```term\n$ git log --oneline -n 10                 # last ten commits\n$ git log --since=\"2 weeks ago\" --until=\"yesterday\"\n$ git log --author=\"Joel\"                 # by author (a regex: partial names work)\n$ git log --grep=\"shipping\"               # by words in the message\n$ git log -S \"freeShipping\"               # commits that added/removed this text (\"pickaxe\")\n$ git log -- shipping/rates.json          # only commits touching this path\n$ git log --stat                          # files changed per commit\n$ git log -p -2                           # full diffs of the last two commits\n$ git log main..feature                   # what feature has that main does not\n$ git log --format=\"%h %an %ar %s\"        # your own format\n```\n\nFormat placeholders worth knowing: `%h` short id, `%H` full id, `%an` author name, `%ar` relative date, `%ad` date, `%s` subject (first line).\n\nFilters combine. `git log --author=Joel --since=\"1 month ago\" -- shipping/` reads like a sentence."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A detective's toolkit","md":"A detective rarely reads every file in the archive. They filter: *who* was there, *when*, *what* was touched, *which words* appear in the statement. `git log` filters work the same way:\n\n- `--author` is \"who was there\"\n- `--since` / `--until` is \"when\"\n- `-- path` is \"what was touched\"\n- `--grep` searches the statements (messages)\n- `-S` searches the evidence itself (the code)"},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"History on a real team","md":"- **Incident reviews** start with \"what changed recently?\": `git log --since=\"2 days ago\" --oneline`.\n- **Onboarding**: reading `git log --oneline -- src/payments` shows how a module evolved and who knows it best.\n- **Release notes**: `git log v1.3.0..v1.4.0 --oneline` lists everything shipped between two releases.\n- **Code review**: `git log main..my-branch` shows exactly what your pull request contains.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Create an alias for the view you use most: `git config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --all\"`."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Log pitfalls","md":"**Forgetting `--` before a path that looks like a branch name.** `git log -- main.py` is unambiguous; use `--` when in doubt.\n\n**`--grep` searches messages, not code.** To search code changes, use `-S`.\n\n**`--author` matches name or email**, partial and case-sensitive by default. `--author=joel` might not match \"Joel\".\n\n**`HEAD~2` vs `HEAD^2`.** The first means \"grandparent\"; the second means \"second parent of a merge\". Mixing them up gives confusing results on merge commits."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find the shipping-fee culprit","md":"The bookshop repository has a few weeks of history from several people. Use log filters to answer the questions. The **Commit graph** panel helps you check your answers."}},"time-travel/reflog":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The branch that vanished","md":"Diego deleted what he thought was an old branch:\n\n```term\n$ git branch -D payment-retries\nDeleted branch payment-retries (was 5e8a1c3).\n```\n\nIt was not old. It held two days of unpushed work. His stomach dropped. A senior colleague leaned over:\n\n```term\n$ git reflog\na10d2e4 HEAD@{0}: checkout: moving from payment-retries to main\n5e8a1c3 HEAD@{1}: commit: Retry failed payments with backoff\n...\n$ git branch payment-retries 5e8a1c3\n```\n\nThe branch was back, commits and all. Git had never deleted the commits, only the label pointing at them. And git keeps a private diary of every position HEAD has been at: the **reflog**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Commits are hard to lose","md":"Git almost never deletes commits immediately. When you reset, amend, rebase or delete a branch, the commits are simply no longer *reachable* from a branch. They stay in the object database for at least 30 days (by default) before garbage collection may remove them.\n\nThe reflog records every move of HEAD (and of each branch) **on your machine**:\n\n```term\n$ git reflog\n9fba052 (HEAD -> main) HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to HEAD~2\ne90e551 HEAD@{1}: commit: Add invoice totals\n3c4a1b0 HEAD@{2}: commit: Add invoice table\n98969a4 HEAD@{3}: checkout: moving from feature to main\n```\n\n`HEAD@{1}` means \"where HEAD was one move ago\". You can use it anywhere you would use a commit id: `git reset --hard HEAD@{1}` undoes the reset you just did.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Recovering from reset --hard\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add invoices\"; commit \"Add invoice table\"; commit \"Add invoice totals\"\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD~2 | Oops. main jumped back two commits. The lost commits are drawn faded: unreachable, but still stored.\nstep: git reflog | The reflog lists every position HEAD has had, including before the reset.\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD@{1} | Jump back to where HEAD was one move ago. Everything is restored.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Recovery recipes","md":"```term\n$ git reflog                          # every move of HEAD, newest first\n$ git reflog show main                # moves of one branch\n$ git reset --hard HEAD@{1}           # undo the last reset / amend / rebase\n$ git branch rescued <id>             # give a lost commit a branch again\n$ git switch -c rescued <id>          # same, and switch to it\n$ git cherry-pick <id>                # copy one lost commit onto the current branch\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The building's CCTV","md":"Branches are like name tags on desks. Remove a name tag and the desk (the commit) is still there; you just cannot find it by name. The reflog is the building's CCTV footage: it shows every desk you visited, in order, with timestamps. Find the moment in the footage, walk back to that desk, and put a name tag on it again."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What the reflog can and cannot do","md":"- It is **local only**. It is not pushed and does not exist on GitHub or on a teammate's machine.\n- It records **commits**, so it cannot recover uncommitted edits destroyed by `reset --hard` or `restore`. Commit early, even as \"WIP\", and squash later.\n- Entries expire (90 days for reachable, 30 for unreachable by default).\n- It is the reason senior developers seem fearless with git: they know that anything **committed** is almost always recoverable."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Reflog misunderstandings","md":"**\"I'll recover it from GitHub's reflog.\"** There isn't one you can use. Recover on the machine where the work happened.\n\n**Searching the wrong reflog.** `git reflog` is HEAD's log. If you are looking for a branch's old position, `git reflog show <branch>` can be clearer.\n\n**Using `HEAD@{n}` after more moves.** Each new move shifts the numbers. Use the commit id from the reflog line to be safe."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo the undo","md":"A careless afternoon: a hard reset too far and a deleted branch. Use the reflog to recover both, without losing anything."}},"time-travel/reset":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three commits too many","md":"Aisha spent the morning on a new checkout flow and made three commits. At lunch her team lead said the product team had cancelled the feature. The commits were never pushed.\n\nOption one: delete the code by hand and make a fourth commit. But the history would then contain three commits for a feature that never existed, plus an undo.\n\nOption two:\n\n```term\n$ git reset --hard HEAD~3\nHEAD is now at 4ab9e10 Fix footer links\n```\n\nThe branch jumped back three commits and her files matched that commit again. Her unpushed history was as if the morning never happened.\n\n`git reset` moves the current branch to another commit. Its three modes decide what happens to your staging area and your files along the way. Understanding them is the difference between a precise undo and losing a day of work."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What each mode resets","md":"```diagram\nreset-modes\n```\n\n`git reset <commit>` always **moves the current branch** (and HEAD) to `<commit>`. Then:\n\n| Mode | Staging area | Working directory | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `--soft` | untouched | untouched | Undo commits but keep everything **staged**: re-commit differently, squash several commits into one |\n| `--mixed` (default) | reset to match `<commit>` | untouched | Undo commits and **unstage**: keep the edits as normal changes |\n| `--hard` | reset | **reset**: files overwritten | Throw commits **and** uncommitted edits away |\n\n```replay\ntitle: Soft, mixed and hard on the same history\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add cart\"; commit \"Add checkout step 1\"; commit \"Add checkout step 2\"\nstep: git reset --soft HEAD~1 | --soft: main moves back one commit. The undone change is still staged.\nstep: git reset HEAD~1 | --mixed (the default): main moves back another commit and the staging area is reset. Both changes are now unstaged edits in the working directory.\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD | --hard: the staging area and working directory are reset to HEAD. The edits are gone.\n```\n\nWithout a commit, `git reset` defaults to `HEAD`, so `git reset` alone just unstages everything, and `git reset file.txt` unstages one file (like `git restore --staged`)."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reset recipes","md":"```term\n$ git reset --soft HEAD~1      # undo the last commit, keep its changes staged\n$ git reset HEAD~1             # undo the last commit, keep its changes unstaged\n$ git reset --hard HEAD~1      # undo the last commit and delete its changes\n$ git reset --hard             # discard ALL uncommitted changes (back to HEAD)\n$ git reset --soft HEAD~3      # squash: then commit once to replace three commits\n$ git reset --hard origin/main # make your branch identical to the remote one\n$ git reset app.js             # unstage one file\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Rewinding a tape","md":"Imagine recording music on a multi-track tape:\n\n- `--soft` rewinds the tape counter but leaves the recorded sound sitting in the mixing desk, ready to be re-recorded.\n- `--mixed` rewinds and clears the mixing desk, but the musicians still have their parts written on their sheet music.\n- `--hard` rewinds, clears the desk **and** tears up the sheet music."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Reset on a team","md":"Reset rewrites **your branch's history**. The rules match amend:\n\n- Reset freely on **local, unpushed** commits.\n- On a branch others have pulled, use `git revert` (next lesson) instead.\n- `git reset --hard origin/main` is a common \"make my local main match the server exactly\" command, e.g. after a messed-up experiment on main.\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> `git reset --hard` deletes uncommitted work with no confirmation and no recycle bin. Committed work can usually be recovered with the reflog; uncommitted edits cannot. Run `git status` first."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Reset traps","md":"**`--hard` with uncommitted work you wanted.** Gone. Stash first if unsure.\n\n**Resetting a pushed branch.** Your next push is rejected (non-fast-forward). Forcing it rewrites history for everyone.\n\n**Confusing reset and revert.** Reset moves the branch backwards (history disappears). Revert moves forwards by adding an undo commit (history kept).\n\n**Forgetting the reflog exists.** \"I reset too far!\" is fixable: `git reflog`, find the old id, `git reset --hard <id>`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo at three strengths","md":"A shopping-cart project has some commits you want to undo in different ways. Watch the **Three areas** and **Commit graph** panels after each reset: the graph keeps showing undone commits faded, because the reflog still remembers them."}},"time-travel/revert":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Friday's bug, Monday's fix","md":"A bad commit reached `main` on Friday and was deployed. Since then, four teammates have pulled `main` and two more commits have been merged on top. Rewriting history with reset now would pull the floor out from under everyone.\n\nInstead, the team lead ran:\n\n```term\n$ git revert 9fba052\n[main 6546696] Revert \"Change checkout button handler\"\n 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)\n```\n\n`git revert` creates a **new** commit that applies the exact opposite of the bad one. Nothing is erased: history now honestly says \"this was added, and later undone.\" Everyone simply pulls the new commit."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Undo by adding","md":"```replay\ntitle: Revert adds an inverse commit\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add login\"; commit \"Change button colour\"; commit \"Add reviews\"\nstep: git revert --no-edit HEAD~1 | A new commit undoes \"Change button colour\". The commits after it are untouched and history only moves forward.\n```\n\n| | `git reset` | `git revert` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Direction | Moves the branch **back** | Adds a commit **forward** |\n| History | Rewritten (commits drop off) | Preserved, plus an undo commit |\n| Safe on shared branches? | No | Yes |\n| Can undo an old commit in the middle? | Not without also dropping everything after it | Yes, just that one |\n\nIf later commits changed the same lines, revert can produce a **conflict**, just like a merge. You resolve it, then run `git revert --continue`. Conflicts are covered in the Merging realm."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Revert recipes","md":"```term\n$ git revert <commit>              # undo one commit (opens the editor for the message)\n$ git revert --no-edit <commit>    # use the default \"Revert …\" message\n$ git revert HEAD                  # undo the latest commit\n$ git revert A..B                  # undo a range of commits (one revert commit each)\n$ git revert --no-commit <commit>  # apply the inverse but let me commit it myself\n$ git revert --abort | --continue  # control a revert that hit a conflict\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Correcting a published newspaper","md":"A newspaper never un-prints yesterday's edition. If an article had an error, today's edition carries a correction: \"Yesterday we reported X; in fact Y.\" Readers of both editions end up with the truth, and the record stays honest.\n\nReset is pulping yesterday's print run. Fine if nobody has bought a copy yet; a scandal if they have."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Revert in practice","md":"- **Production incident**: the fastest safe fix is often \"revert the commit that caused it, deploy, then investigate calmly.\"\n- On GitHub, merged pull requests have a **Revert** button that opens a new PR containing the revert commit.\n- Reverting a **merge commit** needs `-m 1` to tell git which parent is the mainline. You will see it again in the Merging realm."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Revert traps","md":"**Reverting the wrong commit.** Check `git show <id>` first.\n\n**Reverting a revert.** Undoing a revert brings the original change back. It works, but leave a clear message.\n\n**Expecting revert to remove sensitive data.** The bad commit is still in history. For leaked secrets, rotate the secret."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo a bad commit in shared history","md":"A team's `main` branch contains a bad commit somewhere in the middle, with good work after it. Undo just that commit without rewriting history."}},"time-travel/time-travel-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Bad Friday","md":"**The scenario.** It is Friday afternoon at *Pinecone*, a small company that sells a recipe app. You inherited a messy local repository from a colleague who left in a hurry. Before the weekend, you must leave it in a clean, correct state.\n\nHere is what you know:\n\n- A commit on `main` from last week introduced a bug (it set the servings calculation to divide by zero). Other people's work came after it, and `main` has already been pushed and pulled by the whole team.\n- Your colleague's last local commit (not pushed) has a typo in its message and is missing a file that is sitting untracked in the folder.\n- Your colleague deleted a branch called `ingredient-search` that held unpushed work the product manager now wants.\n- There is also a throwaway \"debug\" commit on top that must disappear, but it was never pushed.\n\nPlan before you act: which undo is right for **shared** history, and which for **local** history?\n\n| Situation | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Undo a pushed commit | `git revert` |\n| Fix the last unpushed commit | `git commit --amend` |\n| Drop unpushed commits | `git reset` |\n| Recover deleted or \"lost\" work | `git reflog`, `git branch <name> <id>` |\n| Find the bad commit | `git log -S`, `git log --grep`, `git show` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Time Travel gave you","md":"- Name any commit: ids, `HEAD~n`, `HEAD^2`, branches, tags, `HEAD@{n}`.\n- Search history with `git log` filters: `--author`, `--since`, `--grep`, `-S`, `-- path`.\n- Visit the past with `git switch --detach` / `git checkout <commit>`; save experiments with `git switch -c`.\n- `git commit --amend` replaces the last commit.\n- `git reset --soft | --mixed | --hard` moves the branch back with increasing force.\n- `git revert` undoes safely by adding an inverse commit.\n- `git reflog` remembers every position: committed work is almost never truly lost.\n\n**The rule that ties it together:** rewrite only history that is still private to you. Once it is shared, add new commits instead."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Leave the repo clean before the weekend","md":""}}};
